🚀 Week 5 – Day 4 Java Exception Handling & Checked vs Unchecked Exceptions ☕

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Exceptions are events that occur during program execution and disrupt the normal flow of a program.

👉 Exception Handling helps:

✔ Prevent program crashes
✔ Handle errors gracefully
✔ Improve application reliability

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚠️ 1. What is an Exception?

An exception is an abnormal condition that occurs during runtime.

---

📌 Example

```java id="e1"
int a = 10;
int b = 0;

System.out.println(a / b);
```

---

❌ Output

```text id="e2"
Exception in thread "main"
java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero
```

---

🎯 Why Handle Exceptions?

✔ Prevent unexpected termination
✔ Improve user experience
✔ Maintain program stability

---

🔹 2. Exception Hierarchy

```text id="e3"
Object
   │
Throwable
   ├── Error
   └── Exception
         ├── Checked Exceptions
         └── RuntimeException
               └── Unchecked Exceptions
```

---

🛠️ 3. Exception Handling Keywords

Java provides:

* `try`
* `catch`
* `finally`
* `throw`
* `throws`

---

📌 Basic Syntax

```java id="e4"
try {
    // risky code
}
catch(Exception e) {
    // handling code
}
finally {
    // cleanup code
}
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="e5"
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
}
finally {
    System.out.println("Program Ended");
}
```

---

🔹 4. Checked Exceptions

Checked exceptions are checked by the compiler at compile time.

👉 Must be handled using `try-catch` or `throws`.

---

# 📌 Example

```java id="e6"
import java.io.FileReader;

FileReader file =
        new FileReader("data.txt");
```

---

🔍 Common Checked Exceptions

| Exception              | Description        |
| ---------------------- | ------------------ |
| IOException            | Input/Output error |
| FileNotFoundException  | File not found     |
| SQLException           | Database error     |
| ClassNotFoundException | Class not found    |

---

🔹 5. Unchecked Exceptions

Unchecked exceptions occur during runtime.

👉 Compiler does not force handling.

---

📌 Example

```java id="e7"
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};

System.out.println(arr[5]);
```

---

❌ Output

```text id="e8"
ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
```

---

🔍 Common Unchecked Exceptions

| Exception                      | Description               |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------------- |
| ArithmeticException            | Divide by zero            |
| NullPointerException           | Null reference            |
| ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException | Invalid index             |
| NumberFormatException          | Invalid number conversion |

---

📊 Checked vs Unchecked Exceptions

| Feature             | Checked Exception | Unchecked Exception |
| ------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------- |
| Checked by Compiler | ✔ Yes             | ❌ No                |
| Occurs At           | Compile Time      | Runtime             |
| Must Handle         | ✔ Yes             | ❌ No                |
| Parent Class        | Exception         | RuntimeException    |

---

🔹 6. throw Keyword

Used to manually create an exception.

---

📌 Example

```java id="e9"
throw new ArithmeticException("Invalid operation");
```

---

🔹 7. throws Keyword

Used to declare exceptions.

---

📌 Example

```java id="e10"
void readFile() throws IOException {
}
```

---

🔹 8. Multiple Catch Blocks

```java id="e11"
try {
    int[] arr = new int[5];
    arr[10] = 100;
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Arithmetic Error");
}
catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    System.out.println("Array Error");
}
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🎯 Advantages of Exception Handling

✔ Prevents abnormal termination
✔ Separates error-handling code
✔ Improves code readability

---

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Empty catch blocks
❌ Catching generic `Exception` unnecessarily
❌ Ignoring exception messages

---

🧠 Real Insight

👉 In real-world applications, exception handling is used in:

* File operations
* Database connectivity
* Network programming
* Web applications

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📚 Key Takeaways

✔ Exceptions handle runtime errors gracefully
✔ Checked exceptions are verified by compiler
✔ Unchecked exceptions occur during runtime
✔ `try`, `catch`, `finally` manage exceptions
✔ `throw` and `throws` help create and declare exceptions


